---
// Help: the badge (src/pages/badge.svg.ts; the Contribute section's snippets; docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md,
// "The badge").
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const site = new URL("/", Astro.site).href;
const badge = new URL("/badge.svg", Astro.site).href;
const page = `${site}paper/<your paper>/`;
const snippets = [
  { label: "Markdown (a README.md)", text: `[![${SITE_NAME}](${badge})](${page})` },
  { label: "reStructuredText (a README.rst)", text: `.. image:: ${badge}\n   :target: ${page}\n   :alt: ${SITE_NAME}` },
  { label: "HTML", text: `<a href="${page}"><img src="${badge}" alt="${SITE_NAME}"></a>` },
];
const toc = [
  { id: "what", text: "What the badge is" },
  { id: "get", text: "Getting your snippet" },
  { id: "github", text: "Adding it on GitHub" },
  { id: "why", text: "Why no pull request is made for you" },
];
---
<Info
  title="The badge"
  description={`The ${SITE_NAME} badge links a paper's code repository to its page: one image, three snippets, added by the author.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/badge/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    The badge, in the README of a paper's code, sends the code's readers to the paper's page on {SITE_NAME}: the paper
    beside the code, its tracing map, its citation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What the badge is</h2>
  <p><img src="/badge.svg" alt={`${SITE_NAME}: paper ↔ code`} /></p>
  <p>
    One image for every paper, <a href="/badge.svg"><code>/badge.svg</code></a>: a flat rectangle with the platform's name
    and "paper ↔ code". What makes it yours is the link around it, to your paper's page. There is no image per paper —
    the site keeps a fixed number of files — and the badge says nothing that could become false.
  </p>

  <h2 id="get">Getting your snippet</h2>
  <p>
    Signed in as a verified author of the paper, or a maintainer of its code, open the paper's page: its Contribute
    section shows "Add the badge to its README" with three snippets, already pointing to the paper's page, each with a
    Copy button. They look like this:
  </p>
  {snippets.map((s) => (
    <>
      <h3>{s.label}</h3>
      <pre>{s.text}</pre>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 id="github">Adding it on GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    For each GitHub repository of the paper, the section links to its README in GitHub's own editor (or to the
    repository when it has none). Paste the snippet at the top, and GitHub offers to commit it, or to open a pull request
    if you cannot write to the repository. Elsewhere, edit the README as you usually do.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why no pull request is made for you</h2>
  <p>
    A one-click pull request would need your GitHub authorization with the <code>public_repo</code> scope, which gives
    write access to every public repository you have. {SITE_NAME} never asks GitHub for any permission: the badge is added
    by you, in GitHub's editor — your consent is in the gesture itself. The <a href="/brand/#badge">brand page</a> gives
    the badge's rules of use.
  </p>
</Info>
